<p>Google Search Console now has a dedicated Generative AI performance report for Search. Google says the report covers AI Overviews and AI Mode, and as of August 31, 2026 the insights had rolled out to websites worldwide.</p>
<h2>What is the Search Console AI performance report?</h2><p>It is a first-party visibility report for generative AI features on Google Search. Instead of treating AI visibility as something you must infer from screenshots or third-party tools, Search Console now exposes impression data for supported generative AI surfaces.</p>
<h2>What does the report measure?</h2><p>Google says the report includes impressions for AI Overviews and AI Mode, with dimensions such as page, country, device and date. An impression means a link from your site was shown to a user in a supported generative AI feature.</p>
<h2>What does the report not tell you?</h2><p>The report is not a replacement for the standard Performance report. It is focused on visibility in the generative-AI surfaces and should be read alongside normal clicks, impressions, queries, pages and analytics data. It also does not tell you everything about the wording or position of a citation inside an AI response.</p>
<h2>How should you read an increase in AI impressions?</h2><p>An increase means your links were shown more often in the supported AI surfaces. It does not automatically mean more clicks, conversions or revenue. The next step is to compare pages gaining AI visibility with downstream engagement and business outcomes.</p>
<h2>How should you investigate an AI visibility drop?</h2><ol><li>Check the report's date range and dimensions.</li><li>Check Google's Search Console data-anomalies page.</li><li>Compare the period with the standard Web Performance report.</li><li>Review major site changes, indexing issues and content changes.</li><li>Compare page-level patterns rather than reacting to the site total alone.</li></ol><p>Google has already documented a 2026 logging error that temporarily reduced reported Generative AI impressions, which is a good reminder to validate the data before changing strategy.</p>
<h2>How is AI visibility different from traditional SEO reporting?</h2><p>Traditional SEO often centers on queries, rankings, clicks and CTR. Generative AI visibility adds another layer: whether a page is being surfaced as a supporting source inside an AI-generated response. The reporting unit is different, so your dashboard language should be precise.</p>
<h2>How can you connect the report to SEO work?</h2><p>Group pages by topic, intent and template. Look for pages that earn AI impressions and inspect what makes them useful: direct answers, unique data, original experience, clear structure or strong topical coverage. Then compare those patterns with pages that receive little or no AI visibility.</p>
<h2>Should you stop using third-party AEO tools?</h2><p>No. Search Console gives you Google-owned impression data, while third-party tools can answer other questions such as prompt tracking, citation position, brand mentions across several AI engines and competitor visibility. The two datasets solve different measurement problems.</p>
<h2>What is a practical AI-search dashboard?</h2><p>Track four layers: Google generative-AI impressions, standard organic clicks and impressions, analytics engagement, and business conversions. Keep a separate annotation log for algorithm updates, site launches, content changes and known Search Console anomalies.</p>
